---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import { RESOLVING_PROPERTIES, PROPERTIES_PROBED_AT } from '../../data/canonical_properties';
import { buildWebPageJsonLD } from '../../utils/seo';
import {
  vaultCount,
  edgeCount,
  connectedCount,
  unconnectedCount,
  statusCounts,
  recordedRepoCount,
  subnetworkCount,
  subnetworksWithPublicUrl,
  registryGeneratedAt,
  subnetworksGeneratedAt,
} from '../../data/network_state';

// HAUSSMANN P1.2 — the dated state-of-the-network disclosure surface. Instrument §8.3 calls this the
// campaign's single highest-leverage move: the site's greatest asset is its honesty, and its greatest
// risk is the gap between the scale it depicts and the scale a skeptical reader can verify. §7.3 is
// explicit that the operator-federation distinction must be made ON THE PAGE, in the reader's line of
// sight — a hostile reader will check, and finding it themselves costs you the account.
//
// EVERY NUMBER HERE IS DERIVED (network_state.ts → the committed data files). Nothing is typed. If you
// are editing this page and find yourself about to write a digit, stop: add it to network_state.ts and
// the gate-20 claim-trace manifest instead. That coupling is the reason the page can claim "no number
// on this page is typed by hand" and have it be true rather than aspirational.
//
// The zero-verified-URL fact is stated as FIXED PROSE rather than a rendered count, because the grammar
// has to change when the count stops being zero. Gate-20 pins the underlying value at 0 and goes red the
// moment it changes — deliberately, so copy and count update in the same diff.
// The repository THIS SITE is built from — distinct from REPO_HTTPS (the clone-and-run image).
// A reader checking this page's numbers, or reporting that one is wrong, needs the vault the site
// and its data files actually live in; sending them to the image would send them somewhere the
// files are not. Declared as a canonical property, and allowlisted in gate-14 for this page.
const SITE_REPO = 'https://github.com/aDNA-Network/aDNA.aDNA';
const issuesUrl = `${SITE_REPO}/issues`;
const jsonLD = buildWebPageJsonLD({
  title: 'The state of the network — aDNA',
  description:
    'What actually runs, what is operator-operated, what is not ours, and what is only planned — each with the date it was last checked.',
  url: new URL('/state-of-the-network/', Astro.site ?? 'https://adna.network').href,
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The state of the network — aDNA"
  description="An honest, dated account of the aDNA network: what runs, what is operator-operated, what belongs to someone else, and what is only planned. Every number derived; every property checked from outside."
  jsonLD={jsonLD}
>
  <article class="policy">
    <header class="policy-head">
      <h1>The state of the network</h1>
      <p class="policy-lead">
        The honest answer to &ldquo;how big is aDNA?&rdquo; is smaller than the registry looks.
        {vaultCount} vaults sound like {vaultCount} teams. They are not. Here is what actually runs,
        who operates it, what is genuinely not ours, and what is only planned &mdash; each with the
        date it was last checked.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2 id="what-runs">What runs</h2>
    <p>
      Everything in this section is a URL you can open, logged out, on a machine that has never heard
      of us. The date beside each one is the day it was last opened from outside.
    </p>

    <ul class="state-ledger" role="list">
      {RESOLVING_PROPERTIES.filter((p) => p.kind !== 'machine-surface').map((p) => (
        <li class="state-row">
          <h3 class="state-name">
            {p.url.startsWith('/')
              ? <a href={p.url}>{p.label}</a>
              : <a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{p.label}</a>}
          </h3>
          <dl class="state-facts">
            <div class="state-fact">
              <dt>What it is</dt>
              <dd>{p.what}</dd>
            </div>
            {p.ownerNote && (
              <div class="state-fact">
                <dt>Who runs it</dt>
                <dd>{p.ownerNote}</dd>
              </div>
            )}
            <div class="state-fact">
              <dt>Last opened from outside</dt>
              <dd>{p.probed}</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <p>
      That is the whole list. If something is not on it, it is not yet a thing you can check. The
      machine-readable surfaces this site offers, and every property we consider legitimate, are
      listed on <a href="/canonical-properties/" data-astro-prefetch>canonical properties</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="operator-operated">What is operator-operated</h2>
    <p>
      The registry holds {vaultCount} vaults. <strong>All of them run on one computer, operated by
      one person</strong> &mdash; Stanley Bishop, the Founding Architect named on
      <a href="/about/" data-astro-prefetch>Who&rsquo;s behind aDNA</a>. The graph on the home page is
      a real graph of real, declared relationships. It is not evidence of adoption, and this site will
      not present it as any.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a real single point of failure, and it is a bounded one. The standard is MIT-licensed,
      the workspace it lives in is a public git repository, and nothing here runs on a server we
      control &mdash; a vault is plain Markdown files on your own machine. If this operator stopped
      tomorrow, every vault would keep working and anyone could fork the standard unchanged.
    </p>
    <p>What the registry records about itself, as regenerated on {registryGeneratedAt}:</p>
    <ul class="state-bullets">
      <li>
        {connectedCount} vaults are joined by {edgeCount} declared relationships.
        {unconnectedCount} declare none &mdash; that is honest topology, not missing data.
      </li>
      {/* Built as one string, not a JSX fragment map: the literal space between {s.count} and
          {s.label} is collapsed by the compiler, which rendered "57genesis, 10pending". */}
      <li>By stage: {statusCounts.map((s) => `${s.count} ${s.label}`).join(', ')}.</li>
      <li>
        {recordedRepoCount === 1 ? 'Only one vault records' : `Only ${recordedRepoCount} vaults record`}
        a code repository at all, and it does not resolve publicly &mdash; so this site does not link
        it.
      </li>
      <li>
        No vault page carries an externally verified public URL. The check is built and running: a
        URL cannot appear on a vault page until someone has opened it from outside, logged out, and
        recorded the date. None has passed yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        Most vaults are tended by a named agent. Those are AI personas, not people;
        <a href="/about/" data-astro-prefetch>the about page</a> says so plainly and explains why.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Of the {subnetworkCount} subnetworks on <a href="/commons/" data-astro-prefetch>the commons</a>,
      {subnetworksWithPublicUrl} have a public property you can open today. Their records were last
      synced on {subnetworksGeneratedAt} &mdash; older than the registry above, and shown rather than
      hidden. Those properties are not a second operator: the one live subnetwork site is run from
      within this same orbit, and its vault is one of the {vaultCount} counted above.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-ours">What is not ours</h2>
    <p>These belong to someone else, and naming them is the point.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Wilhelm Foundation</strong> &mdash; a rare- and undiagnosed-disease foundation, and
      aDNA&rsquo;s anchor partner. It is a separate organisation and no part of this network runs on
      it. <strong>Related-party disclosure:</strong> the person who operates this network also holds
      a role at the Foundation, so treat the relationship as a close one, not as an independent
      third party vouching for us. The Foundation has published no statement about aDNA.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Rare Archive</strong> &mdash; hosted in the Foundation&rsquo;s own GitHub
      organization under Apache-2.0. Not ours to move, and the only public code any subnetwork here
      can show you. As of the last check on {PROPERTIES_PROBED_AT}, every commit in it came from the
      same person who operates this network. We would rather tell you that than let you find it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The hosts</strong> &mdash; this site is static files on Vercel and the code is on
      GitHub. Neither vouches for us. Fonts are served from this domain, so no third party sees your
      visit. <a href="/privacy/" data-astro-prefetch>What this site collects: nothing.</a>
    </p>

    <h2 id="planned">What is planned</h2>
    <Callout variant="info" title="The horizon">
      <p>
        None of this exists yet. It is here so you can tell the difference between what we have and
        what we intend.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A second independent node.</strong> Everything in the registry is on one machine.
          The federation code is written and in the public repository; it has not yet been exercised
          between two operators, so treat it as untested until it has been.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verified external properties.</strong> The check described above is built and
          running; no vault URL passes it today. What is missing is vaults with public homes, not
          machinery.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Profiles, follows, and feeds.</strong> Named as unbuilt on
          <a href="/commons/#today" data-astro-prefetch>the commons</a> &mdash; still unbuilt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The coordination layer, opening progressively.</strong>
          <a href="/reference/specification/" data-astro-prefetch>The specification</a> is the public
          part today.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Nothing above carries a date, because we do not have one. When one becomes true it moves up
        this page, and the date moves with it.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="how-to-check">How to check this page</h2>
    <p>
      Every count above is read at build time from a file in the public repository this site is built
      from &mdash;
      <a href={`${SITE_REPO}/blob/main/site/src/data/vaults.json`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>site/src/data/vaults.json</code></a>
      (regenerated {registryGeneratedAt}),
      <a href={`${SITE_REPO}/blob/main/site/src/data/subnetworks.json`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>site/src/data/subnetworks.json</code></a>
      (synced {subnetworksGeneratedAt}), and
      <a href={`${SITE_REPO}/blob/main/site/src/data/verified_links.json`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>site/src/data/verified_links.json</code></a>.
      <strong>No number on this page is typed by hand.</strong> Every property under &ldquo;what
      runs&rdquo; was opened from outside, logged out, on the date shown beside it. If you check one
      and find it wrong,
      <a href={issuesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue</a> &mdash; that is a bug in
      this page, not a difference of opinion.
    </p>

    <h2 id="where-next">Where to go from here</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>If this is the answer you were hoping for:</strong> the standard works the same whether
      the network has {vaultCount} vaults or several thousand &mdash; it is a way to organise files
      on your own machine, and it does not need anyone else&rsquo;s participation to be useful.
      <a href="/get-started/" data-astro-prefetch>Clone it and see &rarr;</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If you want to be the second independent node:</strong> that is the one thing on the
      horizon above we cannot build alone.
      <a href="/community/" data-astro-prefetch>The community space is here &rarr;</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/vaults/" data-astro-prefetch>Browse the registry &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href="/canonical-properties/" data-astro-prefetch>Canonical properties &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href="/security/" data-astro-prefetch>Reporting a security issue &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <p class="policy-foot">
      Registry regenerated {registryGeneratedAt} &middot; subnetwork records synced
      {subnetworksGeneratedAt} &middot; properties last checked from outside {PROPERTIES_PROBED_AT}.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Scaffold matches the policy pages (/privacy, /security) — same measure, same rhythm.
     Tokens only: gate-25 fails any hardcoded colour here. */
  .policy {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-16) var(--space-6) var(--space-24);
  }
  .policy-head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-10);
  }
  .policy h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .policy-lead {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .policy h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    margin: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-3);
  }
  .policy p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .policy code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }
  .policy a {
    color: var(--color-link);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .state-bullets {
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
  }
  .state-bullets li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* Property ledger — the /commons `today-facts` idiom (label/value grid that stacks at narrow
     widths). Deliberately NOT a <table>: a two-column table is the 320px overflow class gate-9 and
     gate-29 exist to catch. */
  .state-ledger {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }
  .state-row {
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .state-name {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .state-facts {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-6);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .state-fact {
    display: contents;
  }
  .state-fact dt {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
  .state-fact dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .policy-foot {
    margin-top: var(--space-10);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .state-facts {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0;
    }
    .state-fact dt {
      margin-top: var(--space-2);
    }
  }
</style>
